#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:7
#: 441f0682a974400481921648a90255bf
msgid "Managing Gyro Noise with the Dynamic Harmonic Notch Filters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:9
#: 0dd81543cfaf4c86a341bcde3dbcdbfb
msgid "As discussed under the :ref:`Vibration Damping<common-vibration-damping>` topic, managing vibration in ArduPilot autopilot installations is extremely important in order to yield predictable control of an aircraft. Typically installations utilize mechanical vibration damping for the autopilot, internally or externally, in order to remove the worst of the vibration. However, mechanical damping can only go so far and software filtering must be used to remove further noise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:11
#: 5aa61dce72ed4928af69aef7c4452ee4
msgid "To the autopilot, vibration noise looks like any other disturbance (e.g. wind, turbulence, control link slop, etc.) that the autopilot must compensate for in order to control the aircraft. This prevents optimum tuning of the attitude control loops and decreased performance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:13
#: f20e477e7be44796b37b7cf2313b920b
msgid "ArduPilot provides two filtering mechanisms for noise. Lowpass filters on the accelerometer signals, controlled by the :ref:`INS_ACCEL_FILTER<INS_ACCEL_FILTER>`, and  the gyro signals, controlled by :ref:`INS_GYRO_FILTER<INS_GYRO_FILTER>`, and Harmonic Notch Filters on the gyro signals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:15
#: a5c160bfb6c54eb29415835933f91496
msgid "As discussed in :ref:`common-measuring-vibration` section, there are basically two classes of noise/vibrations: those generated within the bandwidth of the gyros/accelerometer sampling and noise above those frequencies which are \"aliased\" down to within that bandwidth and can cause the \"leans\". The aliased noise must be eliminated at the source with improved mounting or frame rigidity, but the above filters can deal with the other sources, typically generated from the motor/propellers at their rotation frequency and its harmonics."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:17
#: c0d13364257f4e95a1d02990e6614fd6
msgid "For multicopters and QuadPlanes, virtually all vibrations originate from the motor's rotational frequency.  For helicopters and planes, the vibrations are linked to the main rotor/prop speed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:19
#: d2eae60bf826413580da2370c0b04773
msgid "ArduPilot has support for two notch filters whose filter frequency can be linked to the motor rotational frequency for motors, or the rotor speed for helicopters, and provides notches at a primary frequency and its harmonics."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:21
#: b2c36d4f6e6a4add9b19b99f0260e352
msgid "While the lowpass filters can effectively diminish the impact of this noise, having low frequency set points creates a lot of phase lag and therefore reduces how aggressive the tune can be before oscillation occurs, which results in a poorer tune."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:23
#: cd459287e58347fcbceeaeb7b14b17ee
msgid "For the gyro based rate controllers, this reduces their ability to respond to fast disturbances. If the gyro lowpass filter can be set higher, the phase lag induced is lower and the tune can be more aggressive. But this allows more noise and vibration, effectively canceling that gain out. Enabling either one or both of the Harmonic Notch filters gives the ability to target the noise generated by the motors, allowing a higher frequency of the following low pass to be set and therefore allowing tighter tune."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:28
#: 1fd11083e1a1494c88acd316f5326342
msgid "Notch Filter Setup Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:30
#: 158ca0802ad148dcac92e6e97fcca6e3
msgid "Select how the notch center frequency will be controlled. See :ref:`center_freq_control`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:31
#: b68efb9ca3be495d9224d7e06656129b
msgid "If a static notch (not usually recommended), or :ref:`throttle-based<common-imu-notch-filtering-throttle-based-setup>` control is used, the dominant noise frequencies will need to be determined in order to setup the notch. See :ref:`notch_center_freq`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:32
#: f85ced114faf49f091982e52661f39b1
msgid ":ref:`Enable the notch filter<notch_enable>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:33
#: d34f3e9ac913427db27766e3ace040ea
msgid "Setup the selected center frequency control method using :ref:`INS_HNTCH_MODE <INS_HNTCH_MODE>`. Then setup its associated parameters by reading its associated page linked in this section :ref:`center_freq_control`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:34
#: 1f5f7353f0a34786a4d22821f729f181
msgid "After a test flight and log analysis with the notch enabled (See :ref:`notch_check`), the :ref:`number and placement of higher harmonic filters implemented can be adjusted <notch_harmonics>`, the use of the :ref:`multi-notch options<notch_options>`, or even a second harmonic notch set (:ref:`INS_HNTC2_ENABLE<INS_HNTC2_ENABLE>`) can be configured to improve noise reduction effectiveness. The web based `Filter Review Tool <https://firmware.ardupilot.org/Tools/WebTools/FilterReview/>`__ can be used to experiment with parameter changes based on the test flight log to determine best configurations without having to make iterative test flights."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:40
#: 9838d096e6f14d7da6c75eedbb3c8124
msgid "Enabling the Notch Filters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:42
#: 79dc49194b5b48ccaf7c6d18d0045a77
msgid "The harmonic notch is enabled overall by setting :ref:`INS_HNTCH_ENABLE<INS_HNTCH_ENABLE>` = 1 for the first notch, and, if needed,  a second set of harmonic notches using :ref:`INS_HNTC2_ENABLE<INS_HNTC2_ENABLE>` = 1. After rebooting, all the relevant parameters will appear."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:44
#: 4ed0dd83fbef4eb3bc10056c32218403
msgid "Various methods of dynamically adjusting the notch(s) center frequency to track motor speed under different thrust conditions is provided, ie Dynamic Harmonic Notch filtering."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:50
#: da2e72df505a4aec88746d5c3add2b56
msgid "Notch Filter Control Types"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:52
#: 36605d16323a4e83a31ada185fac2dff
msgid "Key to the dynamic notch filter operation is control of its center frequency. There are five methods that can be used for doing this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:54
#: 41d85e82f90145d6bf06698bf6c586e0
msgid ":ref:`INS_HNTCH_MODE <INS_HNTCH_MODE>` = 0. **Static center frequency**. Dynamic notch frequency control is disabled. The center frequency is fixed and is static. Often used in Traditional Helicopters with external governors for rotor speed, either incorporated in the ESC or separate for ICE motors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:55
#: 63e0a230a5ad45339814d71d7d034025
msgid ":ref:`INS_HNTCH_MODE <INS_HNTCH_MODE>` = 1. (Default) **Throttle position based**, where the frequency at hover throttle is determined by analysis of logs, and then variation of throttle position above this is used to track the increase in noise frequency. See :ref:`throttle-based<common-imu-notch-filtering-throttle-based-setup>` for further setup details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:57
#: 8ffe9f2978b14e4e9a6af2d28b4714a9
msgid "this type of notch filter cannot be used in Traditional Helicopters since throttle stick position does not relate to motor throttle, but rather collective and will result in a configuration error being declared at boot. The **Static center frequency** type is usually used since helis usually use constant motor speed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:59
#: aeb629aeb54b474591070d83af56dffd
msgid ":ref:`INS_HNTCH_MODE <INS_HNTCH_MODE>` = 2 (RPM Sensor 1) or 5(RPM Sensor2). **RPM sensor based**, where an external :ref:`RPM sensor <common-rpm>` is used to determine the motor frequency and hence primary vibration source's frequency for the notch. Often used in Traditional Helicopters (See :ref:`Helicopters<common-imu-notch-filtering-helicopter-setup>`) using the ArduPilot Head Speed Governor feature. See :ref:`RPM Sensor<common-rpm-based-notch>` for further setup instructions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:60
#: 3bb17c90e0e54422abfd37650503d27b
msgid ":ref:`INS_HNTCH_MODE <INS_HNTCH_MODE>` = 3. **ESC Telemetry based**, where the ESC provides motor RPM information which is used to set the center frequency. This can also be used for the forward motor in fixed wing flight, if the forward motor(s) ESCs report RPM. This requires that your ESCs are configured correctly to support BLHeli telemetry via :ref:`a serial port<blheli32-esc-telemetry>`. See :ref:`ESC Telemetry<common-esc-telem-based-notch>` for further setup instructions. If :ref:`INS_HNTCH_OPTS<INS_HNTCH_OPTS>`, or :ref:`INS_HNTC2_OPTS<INS_HNTC2_OPTS>` if the second set of notches is enabled, has bit 1 set, then a set of notches for each motor will be created, tracking its RPM telemetry, otherwise, the average frequency of all motors will set the center frequency."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:61
#: 54887314c75b44878bc8bd71d6996a8f
msgid ":ref:`INS_HNTCH_MODE <INS_HNTCH_MODE>` = 4. **In-Flight FFT based**, where a running FFT is done in flight to determine the primary noise frequency and adjust the notch's center frequency to match. This probably the best mode if the autopilot is capable of running this feature. It requires that the autopilot firmware supports it (see :ref:`common-limited_firmware` for GyroFFT feature) and has sufficient cpu power (F7/H7 autopilots). See :ref:`In-Flight FFT <common-imu-fft>` for further setup instructions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:63
#: 9a3709ad18c74a05a9fb6b1b0458823f
msgid "All of the above are repeated, independently, for the second notch and are prefaced with ``INS_HNTC2_`` instead of ``INS_HNTCH_``. The following will explain setup for the first set of notches."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:65
#: 4f9a898c18a64ebda6a26deccd8a870f
msgid "only one filter can be mode 4(FFT)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:71
#: 1511454536f44df9a658f89d784d4022
msgid "Determining Notch Filter Center Frequency"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:73
#: 87c5da3e35a64308b66c528d8439931a
msgid "Before actually setting up a dynamic notch filter, the frequencies that are desired to be rejected must first be determined. This is crucial if a static notch or :ref:`common-throttle-based-notch` is used. While the other methods do not require this knowledge before setting up their parameters, it can still be worthwhile as a comparison point for the post filter activation analysis of the filter's effectiveness."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:75
#: 1a9974af43eb40cdbb4fc334df23ca9f
msgid "Once the noise frequency is determined, the notch filter(s) can be further setup. Historically, :ref:`common-imu-batchsampling` has been used for this (also for slow cpu's like F4-based autopilots), logging short bursts of raw IMU data for spectral analysis."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:77
#: d30147b72b8640ba8e8b16dcad49509c
msgid "As of firmware version 4.5 and later, a better method has been developed using continuous raw IMU data, if the autopilot is H7-based, and using a new web based tool. :ref:`This method is now preferred <common-raw-imu-logging>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:79
#: 680bd437c7934cc9906d04fa44dee6d1
msgid "if you see two noise peaks close together, it may be that your copter has significant yaw imbalance. See the :ref:`Yaw Imbalance section <copter:yaw_imbalance>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:85
#: 67ff7cb6a5c14b5aa81fc3b5ba8f28bb
msgid "Number of Harmonics Filtered"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:87
#: 400218f275b44035bd815cffb91bd33e
msgid "Set :ref:`INS_HNTCH_HMNCS <INS_HNTCH_HMNCS>` to enable multiple harmonic filters centered at up to 16x the base frequency (multiples of the center frequency) notches. For ESC :ref:`INS_HNTCH_MODE <INS_HNTCH_MODE>` tracking, each motor will get a set of these notches. If an octocopter sets up three harmonics, this results in 8 x 3 = 24 notch filters. Enabling triple notches (see below) would result in 72 filters! This would most certainly cause excessive cpu loading and performance issues. Other modes only provide a single set of harmonic notches."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:89
#: a8aa7b1a70d24effba7d4daa94a47743
msgid "Always enable only the number of harmonic notch filters actually required and be especially aware of what is being enabled if using ESC (:ref:`INS_HNTCH_MODE <INS_HNTCH_MODE>` = 3) tracking mode. Enabling too many will result in running out of CPU cycles with unpredictable results. Three harmonics is usually safe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:95
#: f51a6f5ecfb642e891d16f9a62fb4ffb
msgid "Checking Notch Filter Effectiveness"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:97
#: 4fd28f04a8b4428c80465235d1555cac
msgid "Once the notch filter(s) are setup, the effectiveness of them can be checked by again measuring the  frequency spectrum of the output of the filters (which are the new inputs to the IMU sensors). Refer back to the :ref:`common-imu-batchsampling`  or :ref:`common-raw-imu-logging` page for this."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:103
#: 0ef118c05da342b3bdf332b1eb90d3b0
msgid "Multi Notch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:105
#: b77220e3e9e645e78d08bbd2c3fbd27f
msgid "The software notch filters used are very \"spikey\" being relatively narrow but good at attenuation at their center. On larger copters the noise profile of the motors is quite dirty covering a broader range of frequencies than can be covered by a single notch filter. In order to address this situation it is possible to configure the harmonic notches as multiple notches that gives a wider spread of significant attenuation. The configuration is controlled by the :ref:`INS_HNTCH_OPTS <INS_HNTCH_OPTS>` parameter. This is a bitmask parameter and multiple options are possible at the same time, but using bit 0, 1, and bit 4 at the same time should be avoided. Use only one of those in a given configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:108
#: 4e90c8f2934a488fb268436e84b9fd0b
msgid ":ref:`INS_HNTCH_OPTS <INS_HNTCH_OPTS>` Bit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:108
#: 78107b04fe754eb090565a869af5032a
msgid "Action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:110
#: 8c609e05518546088051f14889a4c917
msgid "0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:110
#: 607cee782f364a5abaf3ad63b3848306
msgid "Double overlapping Notches"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:111
#: bbbe6c0921aa440bb808843ed6777323
msgid "1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:111
#: 3d2e4d1b27aa442cbbb507a9928a6d17
msgid "MultiSource: if using FFT Mode, the three largest noise sources will have a notch assigned. If ESC Telemetry Mode, then each motor will have a notch assigned at its RPM. If throttle mode, then each motor will have a notch assigned at its throttle input rather than the average overall throttle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:112
#: d88e1c6c12e8484f82e7e9e14290134d
msgid "2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:112
#: 7d8875be79fa47779e5780c4651af337
msgid "Updates the filters at the loop rate. This is cpu intensive, but tracks noise variations faster. Only valid if frequency source updates at loop rate, ie Bi-Directional DShot telemetry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:113
#: af0c71f41ed745d0b280271fabe12fb8
msgid "3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:113
#: 3d9adc3cba64426dbdf706177e162d9e
msgid "Enables notches on every IMU instead of just the primary. This is cpu intensive, but allows better lane switching decisions in noisy situations and for debugging. Not recommended for F4 boards."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:114
#: d1729636741f4d32813fa28ab447336e
msgid "4"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:114
#: 628e0c7382ee4085900b65a650ee6b14
msgid "Triple overlapping Notches"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:115
#: d697f323a9094ad595688cfacfd0ca8c
msgid "5"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:115
#: 1faa2ed2063a47cd8a8dfcae306c06de
msgid "If the RPM source fails (Sensor,ESC telem,FFT) the minimum frequency (:ref:`FFT_MINHZ<FFT_MINHZ>`, :ref:`INS_HNTCH_FREQ<INS_HNTCH_FREQ>`, or :ref:`INS_HNTC2_FREQ<INS_HNTC2_FREQ>`) will be used for notch center frequency"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:118
#: 104f64af4d3d42a3b47c0e05dc7c40a9
msgid "double notch option is no longer recommended since the triple notch option has been added. With a double notch, the maximum attenuation is either side of the center frequency, so on smaller aircraft with a very pronounced peak their use is usually counter productive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-notch-filtering.rst:121
#: f6b51226055744f1a2512a9a6cda1e1e
msgid "Each notch has some CPU cost so if you configure multiple notches you can end up with many notches on your aircraft. For example, triple single (no harmonics) notches, using ESC telemetry will result in 3 notches per motor or 12 total notches. For example, with F4 cpus this should be acceptable, but enabling a second group of triple notches with :ref:`INS_HNTC2_ENABLE<INS_HNTC2_ENABLE>` or multiple harmonic notches, could cause problems."
msgstr ""
